      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This unit-test-only adapter must implement TermLike's horizontal\n movement callback. Indicatif's single-line renderer never calls it;\n the no-op does not participate in application behavior. The PTY\n integration test exercises the actual terminal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` meets the built-in's criteria.\n\n- **Target and form:** The target is an explicit function with a body. The explanation is in fenced citation commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification.\n- **Substance:** It gives a specific reason. The function is a required `TermLike` callback in the test-only `RecordingTerm` adapter. It is a no-op that returns `Ok(())` and has no application behavior.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The body is a no-op, and `RecordingTerm` is declared inside `mod[tests]`. The other `RecordingTerm` callbacks, `move_cursor_down` and `move_cursor_left`, carry the same kind of justification. The claim that indicatif's single-line renderer never calls the function matches the single-line progress bars set up in `Progress::new` and `Progress::with_target`. I did not check this against indicatif's source.\n- **PTY claim:** The commentary says a PTY integration test exercises the real terminal. `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` opens a PTY with `openpty`. It asserts the coverage progress text, such as \"Preparing coverage: recording inputs\" and \"Converting coverage\". It also checks plain output for a dumb terminal.\n\nI found no gap.",
